WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
 What's not to
get nervous about?

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.070
It's the most terrifying
thing most people do--

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.190
is talking in public.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.580
But remember that the
worst that can happen

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.470
is you might be a
little bit embarrassed.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.910
And it can be fixed later.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.633
I think the biggest
lessons I learned--

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.800
if you're dealing with bad
news, get it out quickly.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.690
Just get it out there.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.070
I've interviewed every
president, foreign leaders,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:29.740
politicians, celebrities,
people from all walks of life.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.640
But I think the lessons I've
learned can apply to all of you

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.370
in your conversations with
your friends and your family,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.368
in your conversations
at the workplace.

00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:38.910
And I'm privileged
to have the chance

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.410
to share what I've
learned with you.

00:00:43.045 --> 00:00:44.670
We're going to take
a look back at some

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.650
of my most memorable
interviews--

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.240
some for good
reasons, some for bad.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.430
And I hope that people will
be able to draw lessons

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.500
that they can apply
to their own lives,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.000
how to be both
honest and sensitive,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.150
but how to have hard
conversations, how

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:02.030
to be confrontational
without being a jerk.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.530
In the workplace, nothing is
more important than preparation

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:05.560
before communicating.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.030
If you don't know what you're
talking about, you can't talk.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.960
Back in 2006, I was interviewing
President George W. Bush.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.130
I had the manifestations
of a panic attack.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.230
I was in full flop sweat
in front of the President

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.310
of the United States.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.220
I was absolutely mortified.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.170
And what I learned
by making mistakes

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.210
is that whatever
you're feeling inside,

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.190
it's probably not
as bad as you think.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.740
And it's probably not
showing as much as you think.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.540
You can't have
real conversations

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.970
without candor and honesty.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.750
And that's the heart of
all good communication.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.240
I'm George Stephanopoulos,
and this is MasterClass.

